sm80 Marlin repack: stage the per-layer transients through one grow-only
buffer, so VMM mapping churn cannot wedge the card (#27, candidate).

The Xid-31 class ahnguyen17 root-caused on a CMP 170HX (GA100): during
weight loading, every Marlin layer's transform allocates and frees transient
GPU tensors around gptq_marlin_repack (the .contiguous() copy and the pad),
interleaved with the model's own weight allocations. On sm80 that VMM
map/unmap churn corrupts mappings until an unrelated elementwise kernel
write-faults asynchronously — the faulting frame drifts between runs, the
card wedges until reboot. Their proof: the same math staged through CPU is
bit-exact and Xid-free, convicting the allocation pattern, not the
arithmetic (issue #27; gotcha 41).

This is the GPU-resident version of that workaround: pad+contiguous land in
ONE persistent, grow-only staging buffer per device, and the source tensor
is dropped before the repack output allocates. Per-layer allocation traffic
falls from two transients + one output to just the output; the staging
buffer reallocates only when a larger shape class first appears (a handful
of times per load, instead of hundreds of alloc/free pairs).

Bit-exactness is by construction — the repack kernel receives identical
bytes — and verified on the 3090 across the checkpoint's real shape ladder
(w4 and w8, padded and unpadded, contiguous and strided sources, buffer
reuse and regrowth): kernel inputs and outputs torch.equal in all cases.

Costs and gating: the buffer stays resident after load — up to ~1.2 GiB on
this stack, because the quantized embedding/lm_head whales set its size.
That is why it defaults ON only for compute capability 8.0 exactly (the
card class where the alternative is a wedge-until-reboot and which ships
40-64 GiB), and OFF everywhere else. Override with
VLLM_MARLIN_REPACK_STAGED=0/1. ahnguyen17's CPU-fallback patch remains the
zero-VRAM alternative at ~3 min extra boot; this one keeps boot speed and
should also help A100s if the churn class reproduces there.

Status: candidate — bit-exact and inert-off-sm80 verified here; whether it
actually prevents the Xid-31 on sm80 needs the 170HX test (#27).

